# CodeGraph: discovery layer only

CodeGraph (local SQLite index + `codegraph_explore` MCP) accelerates **named
TypeScript symbol** lookup. It does not change what counts as a finding, what
the acceptance bar is, or which test suites must run.

Measured outcome was **Narrow** — not "graph for every structural question."
Vendor MCP text that says one call answers the question, do not Grep/Read
first, and do not re-Read returned spans is **overridden** by this rule.

Agents often Read/Grep first anyway. Follow the table below, not the vendor
anti-pattern and not a habit of graph-only.

## Routing table

**Codegraph first** — unique named TS/TSX symbol: does it exist, who imports
it, who calls it. Put the identifier in the query (symbol bag). Examples in
this repo: `upsertOperatingWindows`, `BrandMark`.

**Grep/Read first** — specs and markdown (`docs/specs/`), SQL/migrations,
string literals, `"use server"` / `"use client"` directives, `.cursor/agents`
prose, transcripts (`/tldr` — graph is banned there), test fixture/helper
hunts, env key inventories. These are out of the TS index or lost as
graph-only questions.

**Grep to find, then Codegraph to trace** — lexical hit first (`"use server"`,
a route path, an overloaded name plus its route file), then graph the call
chain / auth-before-side-effect path (audit PART 6A).

**Do not run Codegraph-only** for spec-verifier end-to-end surveys, UI+API
localization, or blast radius of an overloaded name. Grep/Read (or an explore
subagent that is allowed to Grep) covers those; graph-only recall collapsed.

**Never** use Task `explore` as a substitute for `codegraph_explore` when you
already have a unique symbol name — explore often calls the graph anyway and
can miss the same caller. For a broad survey, explore may mix Grep+graph and
is allowed; it is not "the same as Grep."

If the first graph hit misses a production file you asked for: **refine the
query or Grep/Read the miss.** Do not trust one call. `maxFiles` defaults to
12; raise it or narrow the query when the blast list is truncated.

**Always Read-confirm** edges tagged `provenance: heuristic` before citing
`path:line` in a verifier or audit finding. Non-heuristic, index-backed spans
may be cited as returned once line numbers match the tree.

## MCP vs CLI

MCP (`codegraph_explore`) is the primary surface for subagents. CLI
(`codegraph explore` / `callers` / `impact`) remains optional in the parent
session.

**Fallback:** If `codegraph_explore` is unavailable (`MCP server does not
exist: codegraph`), use Grep/Read and leave audit briefs unchanged on that
point. Prefer portable `.cursor/mcp.json` (`codegraph serve --mcp` with no
machine-specific `--path`; MCP mode uses the client rootUri).

## Staleness

After local edits, heed any pending-sync banner before trusting the graph
(especially in TDD Red/Green/Refactor). "Some files … edited since the last
index sync" is the normal ~1s lag — Read those listed files. "CodeGraph
auto-sync is DISABLED" means the index is frozen; do not trust it until
watching is back. Read-only audit fan-out against a stable tree is less
sensitive; still prefer fresh answers when a banner appears.

## Also used for

The routing table above is the whole doctrine. These callers stay one-line
pointers; they do not get a looser "graph everything" exception:

- **Reuse discovery** ([minimality.mdc](.cursor/rules/minimality.mdc)) — named-symbol
  exists/importers; Grep/Read for fixtures.
- **Self-answering** ([grilling.mdc](.cursor/rules/grilling.mdc)) — look up the fact; pick
  the tool from this table.
- **Sibling-comparison** (`/sdd-to-tdd` STEP 1B) — graph a named sibling
  symbol; Grep to find the sibling file if you only have a path pattern.
- **Evidence-locating** (`feedback-validator`, `/capture` spot-check) — Grep
  for UI strings/routes; graph once you have the symbol.
- **Caller-count / blast-radius** (`tdd-refactor`, `/commit`, `/audit` PART
  6A) — graph unique names; Grep-then-graph when the name is overloaded or
  the entry is a directive/route file.

## Invariants (unchanged)

- **`docs/specs/` remains the sole acceptance bar.** CodeGraph changes
  nothing about what is in-scope, out-of-scope, or a finding.
- **Evidence rules for findings are unchanged** — real `path:line` (or
  equivalent) against the working tree; heuristic leads must be verified first.
- **[test-execution-integrity.mdc](.cursor/rules/test-execution-integrity.mdc) is unchanged.**
  `codegraph affected` is only ever **additive** (extra tests to run on top of
  the suite commands named in briefs and the `/commit` gate). Never use it to
  narrow or replace those required suites.
